  "dom_id": "step-q344831",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/be3108802cde61c1ffcc5fdf603e97255b4082541f20f628aedd1a01127c05b7.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x$ that is closest to the point $(3,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =-y+{y}^{2}+9 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-y+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(y)=2y-1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
